{{also|pus}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|po͝os}}, {{IPA|/pʊs/|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|ʊs|lang=en}}

===Etymology 1===
From a Common {{der|en|gem}} word for cat, perhaps ultimately imitative of a sound made to get its attention (compare Arabic [[بسة]]). Akin to {{cog|nl|poes||puss, cat”, slang for “vagina}}, {{cog|fy|poes}}, {{cog|nds|Puus}}, {{m|nds|Puuskatte}}, {{cog|da|pus}}, dialectal {{cog|sv|kattepus}}, {{cog|no|pus}}.

Found also in several other European, North Africa and West Asian languages; compare {{cog|ro|pisică}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|es}}

# {{lb|en|informal}} A [[cat]].
#: {{ux|en|Our local theatre is showing '''Puss''' in Boots.}}
# A [[girl]] or young [[woman]].
# {{lb|en|dated|hunting}} A [[hare]].
#* {{RQ:Fielding Tom Jones}}
#*: He then began to beat about, in the same language and in the same manner as if he had been beating for a hare; and at last cried out, "Soho! '''Puss''' is not far off. Here's her form, upon my soul; I believe I may cry stole away."
# {{lb|en|vulgar|slang}} The [[vulva]] (female [[genitalia]]).
# {{lb|en|vulgar|slang|chiefly|_|North America}} A [[coward]]; a [[wuss]]; someone who is unable to stand up for him- or herself.

=====Synonyms=====
* {{sense|cat}} {{l|en|moggie}}/[[moggy]]

=====Related terms=====
* {{l|en|pussy}}

====Derived terms====
* {{l|en|puss out}}

===Etymology 2===
Of {{etyl|cel|en}} origin, from or akin to {{etyl|ga|en}} {{m|ga|pus||mouth, lip}}, from {{etyl|mga|en}} {{m|mga|bus|}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|es}}

# {{lb|en|slang}} The [[mouth]].
#: ''She gave him a slap in the '''puss'''.''

=====Synonyms=====
* {{sense|mouth}} {{l|en|cakehole}}, {{l|en|gob}}, {{l|en|mush}}, {{l|en|trap}}

===Anagrams===
* {{anagrams|en|PSUs|USPS|USPs|sups}}

